WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.660
During infection by HIV, three enzymes

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.340
play essential roles in the life cycle

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.620
of the virus: Reverse Transcriptase,

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:20.530
Integrase, and Protease.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.170
The first enzyme, HIV Reverse Transcriptase

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.420
copies the viral RNA genome by combining

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.950
individual host nucleotides to build a

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:37.990
complementary DNA strand.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.360
When Reverse Transcriptase finishes this strand,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.370
It will replace the original viral RNA with

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.950
another DNA strand forming a DNA double helix.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:54.180
The second enzyme, HIV Integrase

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.680
inserts the viral DNA into the cell's DNA.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:58.920
 

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.610
The third enzyme, HIV Protease is needed

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.560
at the last step of the viral life cycle

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.750
to cut the HIV polyprotein into its

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:31.970
individual functional parts.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.470
HIV enzymes are targets for drug therapy.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:40.299
Two types of drugs block Reverse Transcriptase.

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:43.700
Drugs like AZT mimic the normal building

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.039
blocks of DNA, but prematurely terminate

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:51.890
the DNA chain. Drugs like nevirapine

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.000
bind on the back side of Reverse Transcriptase,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.729
distorting the enzyme so

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:57.409
that it can no longer perform its

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:01.789
DNA building reaction. Raltegravir binds to

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:04.100
two magnesium ions in Integrase that are

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:05.659
essential for the DNA integration reaction.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.640
Drugs likes the Saquinavir mimic

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.380
the proteins cut by HIV protease,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.600
blocking the maturation of the virus.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.690
Drug resistance is a major problem for HIV

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:30.470
drug therapy.  For instance, when patients

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.269
are treated with drugs that block HIV

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:34.250
Protease, most of the viruses are

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.650
destroyed but rare mutant forms of the

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:37.300
virus survive.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.500
These mutant viruses make a mutated form of the Protease.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:50.860
This Protease resists

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.599
the binding of drugs, but can still cut

00:02:52.599 --> 00:03:00.280
the viral polyprotein.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.530
Over many generations the population of viruses

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.030
builds up additional mutations that

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:07.260
further resist the drug.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.310
The most effective current treatment

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.440
method uses a combination of drugs that

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.420
attack the different enzymes, so that

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.040
some of the drugs will still be active

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.990
even if one of the enzymes

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.530
becomes resistant.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:46.830
 

